From 0000000000000000000000000000000000000000 Mon Sep 17 00:00:00 2001
From: Giuseppe Maggio <jertlok@proton.me>
Date: Fri, 21 Aug 2026 22:40:00 +0200
Subject: [PATCH] firmware: qcom: scm: add the SCM_SVC_PWR reset calls

Qualcomm's secure world owns a small power service, SCM_SVC_PWR (0x09), with
two calls that belong to the system reset path:

  0x01  disable the SPMI PMIC arbiter
  0x02  deassert PS_HOLD

Mainline has never implemented either, because on every SoC for which
qcom,pshold was described so far the bare register write in msm-poweroff is
enough to drop PS_HOLD. It is not enough everywhere: on msm8998 the write to
0x010ac000 is ignored and only the secure-world call takes the platform down.

Add the two calls. Both are made from the restart path with interrupts off, so
both use qcom_scm_call_atomic(); each passes a single zero argument, which
keeps the SMC inside the register-argument window and away from the tzmem
pool. Neither returns anything useful, and the deassert does not return at all
when the firmware honours it, so both are void.

Availability has to be answered before the restart path is entered, because
__qcom_scm_is_call_available() may sleep. Export a query for the PS_HOLD
deassert so a caller can cache the answer at probe time. The arbiter halt is
strictly best-effort and needs no such query.

Signed-off-by: Giuseppe Maggio <jertlok@proton.me>

Assisted-by: LLM
Signed-off-by: Giuseppe Maggio <jertlok@proton.me>
---
 drivers/firmware/qcom/qcom_scm.c       | 57 ++++++++++++++++++++++++++
 drivers/firmware/qcom/qcom_scm.h       |  4 ++
 include/linux/firmware/qcom/qcom_scm.h |  4 ++
 3 files changed, 65 insertions(+)

diff --git a/drivers/firmware/qcom/qcom_scm.c b/drivers/firmware/qcom/qcom_scm.c
index e777b7cb9b12..7af51f5cf872 100644
--- a/drivers/firmware/qcom/qcom_scm.c
+++ b/drivers/firmware/qcom/qcom_scm.c
@@ -875,6 +875,63 @@ int qcom_scm_io_writel(phys_addr_t addr, unsigned int val)
 }
 EXPORT_SYMBOL_GPL(qcom_scm_io_writel);
 
+/**
+ * qcom_scm_deassert_ps_hold_available() - is PS_HOLD owned by the secure world?
+ *
+ * May sleep: __qcom_scm_is_call_available() issues a non-atomic SCM call, so a
+ * caller that needs the answer on the restart path must ask at probe time and
+ * cache it.
+ *
+ * Return: true if the firmware implements the PS_HOLD deassert.
+ */
+bool qcom_scm_deassert_ps_hold_available(void)
+{
+	return __qcom_scm_is_call_available(__scm->dev, QCOM_SCM_SVC_PWR,
+					    QCOM_SCM_PWR_IO_DEASSERT_PS_HOLD);
+}
+EXPORT_SYMBOL_GPL(qcom_scm_deassert_ps_hold_available);
+
+/**
+ * qcom_scm_deassert_ps_hold() - drop PS_HOLD through the secure world
+ *
+ * Takes the platform down. Does not return when the firmware honours it, so
+ * the caller must have a fallback for the case where it does.
+ */
+void qcom_scm_deassert_ps_hold(void)
+{
+	struct qcom_scm_desc desc = {
+		.svc = QCOM_SCM_SVC_PWR,
+		.cmd = QCOM_SCM_PWR_IO_DEASSERT_PS_HOLD,
+		.arginfo = QCOM_SCM_ARGS(1),
+		.args[0] = 0,
+		.owner = ARM_SMCCC_OWNER_SIP,
+	};
+
+	qcom_scm_call_atomic(__scm->dev, &desc, NULL);
+}
+EXPORT_SYMBOL_GPL(qcom_scm_deassert_ps_hold);
+
+/**
+ * qcom_scm_halt_spmi_pmic_arbiter() - stop SPMI traffic to the PMIC
+ *
+ * Dropping PS_HOLD in the middle of an SPMI transaction locks up some PMICs,
+ * so the vendor halts the arbiter first. Best-effort: a firmware without the
+ * call simply returns an error, which is why this has no availability query.
+ */
+void qcom_scm_halt_spmi_pmic_arbiter(void)
+{
+	struct qcom_scm_desc desc = {
+		.svc = QCOM_SCM_SVC_PWR,
+		.cmd = QCOM_SCM_PWR_IO_DISABLE_PMIC_ARBITER,
+		.arginfo = QCOM_SCM_ARGS(1),
+		.args[0] = 0,
+		.owner = ARM_SMCCC_OWNER_SIP,
+	};
+
+	qcom_scm_call_atomic(__scm->dev, &desc, NULL);
+}
+EXPORT_SYMBOL_GPL(qcom_scm_halt_spmi_pmic_arbiter);
+
 /**
  * qcom_scm_restore_sec_cfg_available() - Check if secure environment
  * supports restore security config interface.
diff --git a/drivers/firmware/qcom/qcom_scm.h b/drivers/firmware/qcom/qcom_scm.h
index a56c8212cc0c..17eaf4e2da0b 100644
--- a/drivers/firmware/qcom/qcom_scm.h
+++ b/drivers/firmware/qcom/qcom_scm.h
@@ -113,6 +113,10 @@ int qcom_scm_shm_bridge_enable(struct device *scm_dev);
 #define QCOM_SCM_SVC_INFO		0x06
 #define QCOM_SCM_INFO_IS_CALL_AVAIL	0x01
 
+#define QCOM_SCM_SVC_PWR			0x09
+#define QCOM_SCM_PWR_IO_DISABLE_PMIC_ARBITER	0x01
+#define QCOM_SCM_PWR_IO_DEASSERT_PS_HOLD	0x02
+
 #define QCOM_SCM_SVC_MP				0x0c
 #define QCOM_SCM_MP_RESTORE_SEC_CFG		0x02
 #define QCOM_SCM_MP_IOMMU_SECURE_PTBL_SIZE	0x03
diff --git a/include/linux/firmware/qcom/qcom_scm.h b/include/linux/firmware/qcom/qcom_scm.h
index a55ca771286b..daf961bbf390 100644
--- a/include/linux/firmware/qcom/qcom_scm.h
+++ b/include/linux/firmware/qcom/qcom_scm.h
@@ -83,6 +83,10 @@ bool qcom_scm_pas_supported(u32 peripheral);
 int qcom_scm_io_readl(phys_addr_t addr, unsigned int *val);
 int qcom_scm_io_writel(phys_addr_t addr, unsigned int val);
 
+bool qcom_scm_deassert_ps_hold_available(void);
+void qcom_scm_deassert_ps_hold(void);
+void qcom_scm_halt_spmi_pmic_arbiter(void);
+
 bool qcom_scm_restore_sec_cfg_available(void);
 int qcom_scm_restore_sec_cfg(u32 device_id, u32 spare);
 int qcom_scm_set_gpu_smmu_aperture(unsigned int context_bank);
